🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Data Types in Java

Java data types are classified into two categories:

✅ Primitive Data Types

These store simple values directly.

byte

short

int

long

float

double

char

boolean

Each primitive type has a fixed memory size and is used for efficient data storage.

✅ Non-Primitive Data Types

These store references to objects.

String

Arrays

Classes

Interfaces

Non-primitive types allow storing complex and structured data.

2️⃣ Variables

A variable is a named container used to store data values.

🔹 Syntax:
dataType variableName = value;
🔹 Example Program:
public class DataTypesExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int age = 21;
        double salary = 25000.50;
        char grade = 'A';
        boolean isActive = true;
        String name = "Sangmesh";

        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Age: " + age);
        System.out.println("Salary: " + salary);
        System.out.println("Grade: " + grade);
        System.out.println("Active: " + isActive);
    }
}
🔹 Naming Conventions in Java

To maintain clean and readable code, the following conventions are used:

Variables & Methods: camelCase → studentName, calculateTotal()

Class Names: PascalCase → StudentDetails

Constants: UPPER_CASE → MAX_VALUE

Package Names: lowercase → com.company.project

